{{!-- The edit / delete pair that rides under one GM-prep card, for all three kinds.

      `kind` spells both the class words the sheet's handler table matches on
      (`.<kind>-edit-open` / `.<kind>-remove`, see gm-prep-tabs.js `PREP_TOOLS`) AND what the
      tooltips call the thing. One parameter, not two: every call site passed the same word
      twice, and a pair free to disagree is a working button with the wrong tooltip. Passing
      the kind rather than copying the block per family is what keeps those class words and
      that table in step: a fourth prep kind is one more row in the table and one more `kind=`,
      not two hand-copied markup blocks.

      BOTH BUTTONS ARE ALWAYS LIVE. The trash used to be gated behind the section's edit pencil
      — the panel wore `stonetop-readonly`, and the trash was the one control deliberately left
      off the CSS allow-list, with a matching check in the handler. Neither tab has that pencil
      any more, and the reason is worth keeping: on both tabs the pencil ended up locking this
      one button and nothing else, because everything else on a prep card writes nothing (a card
      is written in its wizard, the d20 tables only roll, the doom ticks are a play-time
      tracker). What a GM saw was a trash sitting beside a live pencil that gave no hover
      feedback and swallowed clicks, with nothing on screen naming the lock. The confirm dialog
      the handler opens is the guard deleting actually needs.

      The pencil keeps its SECOND, kind-free class. `stonetop-prep-edit-btn` is no longer a gate
      word — it is the look, shared with `stonetop-prep-add-btn` one partial over — but it is
      still the thing to hang shared styling on, for the reason that block gives: a rule named
      per kind is a rule a fourth kind falls off silently.

      Any button a kind wears BESIDE those two ("Mark visited", on a site) comes off
      `prepActions`, which this partial reads straight off the card it is drawn under: the prep
      tabs stamp it on every card's view-model from the `actions` column of their `PREP_TOOLS`
      table, and the click handler dispatches off that same column. So no call site passes a
      flag and nothing below names a kind. There was an `#if visit` block here once, switched on by
      `visit=true` from the sites section, and every later per-kind button would have been
      another flag here and another branch in the handler, which is the special-casing this
      header warns about. `../uuid` inside the loop, because `#each` rebinds `this` to the
      action and the hash parameters go with it. --}}
<div class="threat-card-tools">
	{{#each prepActions}}
	<button type="button" class="{{this.cls}}" data-page-uuid="{{../uuid}}"
	        aria-label="{{localize label}}"
	        data-tooltip="{{localize tooltip}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP"><i class="fas {{this.icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i></button>
	{{/each}}
	<button type="button" class="stonetop-prep-edit-btn {{this.kind}}-edit-open" data-page-uuid="{{this.uuid}}" data-tooltip="Edit {{this.kind}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP"><i class="fas fa-pen"></i></button>
	<button type="button" class="{{this.kind}}-remove" data-page-uuid="{{this.uuid}}" data-tooltip="Delete {{this.kind}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP"><i class="fas fa-trash"></i></button>
</div>
